/**
 * \mainpage QAccelPlot API reference
 *
 * QAccelPlot is a hardware-accelerated 2D plotting library for Qt Quick.
 * It provides QML-friendly plot components backed by C++17 and the Qt Scene
 * Graph.
 *
 * This reference documents the public C++ classes that implement the QML API.
 * For installation instructions and task-oriented examples, start with the
 * <a href="../getting-started/">Getting started guide</a>.
 *
 * \section api_quick_start Quick start
 *
 * \code{.qml}
 * import QtQuick
 * import QAccelPlot as QAccelPlot
 *
 * QAccelPlot.Plot {
 *     id: plot
 *
 *     xAxis: QAccelPlot.Axis {
 *         viewportMin: 0
 *         viewportMax: 10
 *         side: QAccelPlot.Axis.Bottom
 *     }
 *
 *     yAxis: QAccelPlot.Axis {
 *         viewportMin: -1
 *         viewportMax: 1
 *         side: QAccelPlot.Axis.Left
 *     }
 *
 *     QAccelPlot.LineCurve {
 *         xAxis: plot.xAxis
 *         yAxis: plot.yAxis
 *         color: "darkorange"
 *         lineWidth: 2
 *
 *         Component.onCompleted: setData([
 *             Qt.point(0, 0),
 *             Qt.point(2.5, 0.8),
 *             Qt.point(5, -0.6),
 *             Qt.point(7.5, 0.4),
 *             Qt.point(10, 0)
 *         ])
 *     }
 * }
 * \endcode
 *
 * The QML \c Plot component is the recommended application-level entry point.
 * It wraps \ref QAccelPlot::QAccelPlot "PlotView", the native plot canvas
 * documented in this reference.
 *
 * \section api_key_types Key types
 *
 * - \ref QAccelPlot::QAccelPlot "PlotView" — plot canvas, layout, navigation,
 *   coordinate conversion, grid, and series collection.
 * - \ref QAccelPlot::Axis — visible ranges, scaling, ticks, labels, panning,
 *   and zooming.
 * - \ref QAccelPlot::LineCurve — GPU-rendered lines, markers, data ingestion,
 *   effects, transitions, and hover handling.
 * - \ref QAccelPlot::RectangleList — hardware-accelerated collections of
 *   data-space rectangles.
 * - \ref QAccelPlot::DataAnchor — positions QML overlays at data coordinates.
 * - \ref QAccelPlot::PlotMouseEvent — mouse positions expressed in plot and
 *   data coordinates.
 *
 * \section api_customization Styling and formatting
 *
 * - Line styles: \ref QAccelPlot::SolidLine, \ref QAccelPlot::DashLine, and
 *   \ref QAccelPlot::NoLine.
 * - Curve effects: \ref QAccelPlot::GradientFill and
 *   \ref QAccelPlot::GradientStroke.
 * - Transitions: \ref QAccelPlot::DrawTransition and
 *   \ref QAccelPlot::MorphTransition.
 * - Tick labels: \ref QAccelPlot::NumericTickLabelFormatter,
 *   \ref QAccelPlot::DateTimeTickLabelFormatter,
 *   \ref QAccelPlot::LogTickLabelFormatter, and
 *   \ref QAccelPlot::TextTickLabelFormatter.
 *
 * \section api_data Feeding curve data
 *
 * Choose the simplest API appropriate for the data source:
 *
 * - Use \c setData() for QML data or conventional C++ point containers.
 * - Use the moved-buffer \c setDataF() overload for large interleaved
 *   \c std::vector<float> buffers produced on the curve's thread.
 * - Use \c postData() to transfer a completed buffer safely from a worker
 *   thread.
 * - Use \c setDataFNoRange() only when axis data ranges are maintained
 *   separately.
 *
 * See the <a href="../performance/">Performance guide</a> for a detailed
 * comparison of the available data paths.
 *
 * \section api_runtime Runtime requirements
 *
 * QAccelPlot requires Qt 6.2 or newer and a hardware Qt Quick Scene Graph
 * backend, such as OpenGL, Direct3D, Vulkan, or Metal. The Qt Quick software
 * backend cannot render the custom curve shaders.
 *
 * Never mutate a QQuickItem directly from a worker thread. Use
 * \ref QAccelPlot::LineCurve::postData "LineCurve::postData()" or queue the
 * operation to the item's thread.
 *
 * \section api_more More documentation
 *
 * - <a href="../concepts/">Concepts and architecture</a>
 * - <a href="../cookbook/">Cookbook</a>
 * - <a href="../performance/">Performance guide</a>
 * - <a href="../troubleshooting/">Troubleshooting</a>
 * - <a href="../faq/">Frequently asked questions</a>
 */
